"""Rules that hold with a relation in the window that they do not touch.

Every rule found by planting a pattern and mutating is stated on a window
holding nothing but the relations it rewrites, because that is what the
description of a link can honestly claim.  It is also why so few of them fire:
of the LNAs a classification still had to search for at n = 8, five in six had
a rule whose left-hand pattern was present and which did not match, because one
more relation was sitting in the window doing nothing (F-023).

These are those rules widened.  `lnaMoves.spectatorExtensions` takes a rule,
puts one untouched relation -- a **spectator** -- somewhere in its window,
growing the window if it has to make room, and `verifyMove` decides whether the
rewrite still holds.  Nothing is searched for and nothing is mutated
speculatively, which is why the whole batch cost eight minutes where discovery
costs hours:

```bash
python discover.py --extend --jobs 4
```

That run generated 10609 widenings of the 368 rules then in the table, kept the
1008 that would fire on an LNA no search had placed, and verified **625** of
them with no failures.  Listed below are the **270** that change the orbit
partition at n <= 9 -- 125 that hold at every window position and 145 that need
an end of the quiver.  The same caveat as `endMoves`: a rule left out is
redundant *at the lengths measured*, and re-running the command gets it back.

What they are worth: with these in, a classification of A_7 needs no search at
all, A_8 needs one for 23 of its 429 rows and A_9 for 392 of 1430 -- 100%, 95%
and 73% placed, against 96%, 81% and 60% without them.

Each entry is (window width in arrows, relations before, relations after,
mutation sequence) and, for the anchored ones, which end.  E-024.
"""

